    "seo": {
      "title": "Boating & Marine Activities in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the evidence behind Qeshm’s marine habitats and Lenj boat traditions, with clear limits on current activity and operator information."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "marine-settings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different ways of encountering the coast",
        "content": "Around Qeshm, marine travel connects several distinct settings. UNESCO’s geopark profile describes coral reefs, mangrove forests, and sandy beaches, and identifies dolphin observation around Hengam Island and snorkelling among reef fish as visitor experiences. These examples establish the area’s range of marine attractions; they do not establish that an excursion is currently operating or suitable under particular conditions. [s1]\n\nThe distinction matters when reading an itinerary. Looking at wildlife from a boat, entering the water, and visiting a boatyard offer different kinds of understanding. The first emphasizes animals and their surroundings, the second underwater habitats, and the third the knowledge through which coastal communities have worked at sea. Qeshm’s appeal lies partly in these connections between natural environments and maritime culture. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hara-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the mangrove environment",
        "content": "Hara is a biosphere reserve associated with the Mehran River delta and the waters near Qeshm. Its defining vegetation belongs to the mangrove genus Avicennia: trees adapted to the coastal environment. UNESCO describes a marine core containing tidal marshes, shallow coves, mangrove forests, and small islands locally known as mud hills. This is a habitat mosaic rather than a uniform stretch of open water. [s2]\n\nThat description gives a mangrove excursion an ecological context. The channels, wooded margins, and shallow waters form parts of a connected environment, rather than simply providing scenery around a boat. UNESCO also identifies the reserve’s aquatic habitats as places used by sea snakes for feeding and breeding. These observations explain the reserve’s biodiversity significance; they are not a prediction of which animals a visitor will encounter. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "lenj-heritage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Lenj boats and knowledge at sea",
        "content": "A Lenj is a traditionally hand-built vessel associated with communities on the northern Persian Gulf coast. UNESCO’s heritage description connects these boats with trading, fishing, sea journeys, and pearl diving. Their significance extends beyond the hull: associated knowledge includes navigation, weather interpretation, boatbuilding, working songs, and oral traditions. [s3]\n\nThe skills were inscribed in 2011 on UNESCO’s List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in Need of Urgent Safeguarding. The inscription account describes an ageing community of practitioners, replacement of wooden vessels by fibreglass boats, and a shift from construction toward repair work. These are documented safeguarding concerns from the heritage account, rather than a newly measured assessment of every boatyard. UNESCO’s Qeshm profile identifies Bandar Laft as a place associated with traditional boatbuilding. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scope-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What this account establishes",
        "content": "Together, the sources support an introduction to Qeshm’s marine habitats and boat traditions. A geopark profile or heritage inscription, however, does not certify a commercial operator’s equipment, staff, insurance, or operating permissions. This page therefore makes no provider recommendation or claim of current departure availability. Its geographic coverage remains concentrated on Qeshm; equivalent research is still needed for other Iranian coasts.",
        "source_ids": []
      }
    ],
