    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-7-9",
    "page_path": "/explore/travel/health-travel/",
    "name": "Health Travel",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/travel/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Health travel brings together journeys for clinical treatment, rehabilitation, and experiences marketed around wellbeing. In Iran, research on international patient services documents coordination and communication challenges, while evidence about particular providers must be assessed separately from destination promotion. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Health travel", "Medical tourism", "Rehabilitation", "International patients", "Patient safety"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:38:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:38:57Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Health Travel in Iran: Treatment, Rehabilitation & Evidence",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s health travel landscape, the distinction between treatment and wellbeing, and the evidence needed to assess international patient services."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A destination category with different purposes",
        "content": "Health travel is useful as an umbrella term, but its branches should remain distinct. Medical tourism means crossing a national border to receive medical care. The CDC identifies dental treatment, cosmetic procedures, fertility care, and cancer treatment among the services sought internationally; this global list is not a verified inventory of services currently available to visitors in Iran. [s1]\n\nThis guide separates medical services, dental services, specialist surgery, rehabilitation, and thermal spa travel. The distinctions direct attention to the purpose of the journey. A diagnostic consultation concerns assessment; an operation involves an intervention and recovery; rehabilitation concerns functioning in everyday life. Treating all of these as a single holiday product can obscure important differences in evidence and continuity of care. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rehabilitation-and-wellbeing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Rehabilitation is more than a recovery setting",
        "content": "WHO describes rehabilitation as interventions that help people function more independently in the context of a health condition and their environment. It can involve mobility, communication, cognition, assistive products, changes to the home, and support for self-management. Its settings include hospitals, outpatient practices, and communities. A pleasant location is therefore only one possible feature of a rehabilitation journey, not its defining clinical component. [s4]\n\nThis distinction is especially useful beside thermal spa travel. The existence of a bathing facility, an appealing landscape, or a wellbeing package does not itself establish a therapeutic effect for a specific condition. No Iran-specific clinical evidence for thermal treatments was verified for this overview. That branch consequently requires separate research into facilities, interventions, and the quality of evidence behind any medical claims.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the service environment",
        "content": "Iran’s health system includes public, private, and charitable hospitals under a framework centrally overseen by the Ministry of Health and Medical Education. The Commonwealth Fund’s country profile also describes geographic differences in specialist provision and barriers created by sanctions and constrained access to medicines. These system-level observations provide context; they cannot establish that a particular hospital has the staff, supplies, or capacity required for a proposed treatment. [s5]\n\nResearch on medical tourism exposes another distinction: attracting international patients and delivering an integrated patient journey are separate tasks. A qualitative study at Iran University of Medical Sciences, based on interviews conducted in 2023–2024, identified concerns involving governance, language skills, payment arrangements, records, and follow-up. These are participants’ accounts from a defined institutional setting, not measured failure rates for every Iranian hospital. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coordination",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The journey extends beyond admission",
        "content": "A separate hospital study, conducted in 2021 across Tehran, Mashhad, Tabriz, Shiraz, Isfahan, and Yazd, reported difficulties involving information systems, financing, staff, equipment, and service coordination. Its authors also identified incomplete follow-up as a concern. Because the study interviewed sector informants rather than patients and companions, it illuminates organizational problems without establishing the experience of every visitor. [s3]\n\nHospital websites show how providers describe their response. Ebnesina Hospital in Tehran advertises an International Patient Department with multilingual assistance and coordination before arrival. This establishes the hospital’s stated service model, not an independent finding about its effectiveness. [s6]\n\nThe CDC’s international guidance emphasizes that treatment, subsequent travel, and recovery interact: surgery and air travel can increase clotting risks, while strenuous holiday activities can interfere with healing. These are general medical-tourism considerations rather than an Iran-specific estimate of complications. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluating-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading claims about the sector",
        "content": "National descriptions, hospital advertisements, qualitative interviews, and clinical outcome studies answer different questions. Together, the inspected sources explain why coordination matters, but they do not support a ranking of Iranian treatment destinations or a claim that lower advertised prices produce better value. The most useful conclusion is to assess health travel as a connected course of care, with responsibilities that continue after the patient leaves the destination. The research reviewed here makes that continuity a central issue. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
